Chapter Ten: "The Country of the Lion King"

Chapter Ten, Part 31: "I'm Home, Welcome Back"

—Come to think of it, Flügel was also a person full of mysteries.

The first time Subaru heard that name was, surely, when he obtained information about the "Great Tree of Flügel." He had heard that, aside from planting that great tree, Flügel was a great person with no other notable achievements to his name—though Subaru's understanding was only at the level of "leaving your name in history is impressive enough."

After that, there was the matter of having cut down that very tree in order to defeat the White Whale, so along with feeling sorry about it, Flügel could also be called a benefactor to whom he was grateful for planting the tree.

But the next time he heard Flügel's name, the suspiciousness suddenly increased—when the Star Keeper of the Pleiades Watchtower, Shaula, spoke of him as her master.

Originally one of the Three Heroes of Lugunica, Shaula had been praised as the "Sage," but Subaru was told that her achievements had actually been forced upon her by Flügel, and for some reason, Shaula kept insisting that Subaru was that master of hers. —In the end, the truth of that matter remains unresolved, leaving Subaru with complicated feelings toward Flügel.

One could say Flügel was a benefactor who gave the final push in the White Whale subjugation battle, and also a terribly heartless person who locked Shaula away in the watchtower for an eternity. If Subaru were allowed to be completely selfish about it, he'd want to claim that if Flügel was like that, then the title of Shaula's master should belong to him.

"Man, I sound so wishy-washy even to myself. ...But when I hear the name Flügel after hearing about Kugelschreiber Castle, it starts to feel like German-language respect too..."

If Kugelschreiber meant ballpoint pen in German, then Flügel should likewise mean "wing" in German. At one point, Subaru had also fervently sought out cool-sounding words and arrived at German. You could call it a classic adolescent phase.

"Like Staubsauger (vacuum cleaner) or Zitronensaft (lemon juice). And not to hide anything, the 'Schwarz' in Natsumi Schwarz also means 'black' in German."

"Even if you say that, I haven't the slightest idea what you're talking about. ...So it means Betty's family home and Subaru's hometown used the same words?"

"That's right. Well, I've got my suspicions that 'Hoshin of the Wastelands' was also from the same hometown as me, so it's not that strange for those kinds of words to be scattered around."

"Even Betty doesn't know the circumstances behind the castle's naming. But if it's Mother, it wouldn't be strange for her to be acquainted with anyone."

At Beatrice's words as she was being held, Subaru shifted her in his arms and let out a heavy sigh with a troubled expression, saying, "Yeah, I guess so."

Certainly, at the tea party in the "Sanctuary," Echidna—the younger Echidna, that is—had said something about how people from all over the world sought knowledge and gathered to meet her. At that moment, since he didn't particularly have anything to ask Echidna, he had let it pass, but now he felt like he had missed a precious opportunity.

"Well, how much of what young Donna said can be trusted is questionable, but she did seem to have pride in showing off her knowledge. Maybe I should have asked about the truth of the world or something?"

"What would Subaru do knowing the truth of the world? Besides, the world isn't exactly lying to you, Betty thinks."

"Well, that's true enough."

He'd said it somewhat offhandedly, but indeed, even if he knew the truth of the world, it would probably be more than he could handle.

Right now, what he wanted was the safety of his companions and a way to contact them, plus a means to save the kingdom, and a way to keep the enemy away forever—something like that. Indeed, there was no role for the truth of the world to play.

At any rate—

"—To think that the trip to Flügel's Great Tree would be approved so easily."

As Subaru muttered that, what appeared as a tiny dot in the distance in his field of vision was the topic of discussion—Flügel's Great Tree—that is, its enormous, enormous stump.

The trunk portion that had been cut down for the White Whale subjugation was subsequently hauled away and, making the most of the opportunity, was apparently utilized as spiritually potent timber for the kingdom's resources. It probably became buildings made from Flügel's Great Tree, active in various places.

The remaining stump became a valuable tourist resource and seems to be fairly bustling; currently, Subaru and the others were in the position of waiting for the area to be cleared of people.

"Until then, nobody even glanced at it just because there was a big tree standing there, but now, reeeally, everyone's so quick to jump on the bandwagon."

The one who called out like that was Ferris, who was likewise at loose ends just like them.

Leaning against the dragon carriage, he lined up next to Subaru and the others gazing at the remains of the great tree, narrowing his yellow eyes and grumbling his impressions about the circumstances around the tree. Subaru shared the same opinion—even though evening had already passed and night was approaching from beyond the sky, the flow of people showed no sign of stopping.

The stump business could be called an unexpected byproduct of the White Whale subjugation.

"Still, I'm surprised that we'd end up coming to Flügel's Great Tree with Subaru-kyun again."

"I didn't think I'd have a part to play here again either. Back in the day, when I heard this spot was treated as a marker for the 'Witch,' I proposed a different plan for the White Whale too."

"Oh? A different plan, like what?"

"Why, squeezing out power with everyone's wisdom and courage that wouldn't lose to cutting down the great tree."

"Aha, amaaazing. Subaru-kyun, you're a geeenius~"

Being told that in a tone that suggested he didn't think so at all, Subaru gave a wry smile while glancing sideways at the other.

Ferris, who had already been acting together with them for two months now, still didn't look familiar in his male attire. If one only counted the time, they'd spent far longer together than when he was in female clothing. He didn't think the reason was simply that it had suited him that well. —The reason why the lingering discomfort remained was something Subaru couldn't quite put into words himself.

"Ferris, are you really coming along too?"

"Hmm~, does Beatrice-chan not like that?"

"It's not about like or dislike. Your power is valuable. If something happened to you..."

"You're worried about me? How sweeet. But no need to worry."

Ferris poked Beatrice's nose with his finger as she was being held in Subaru's arms. At that, Beatrice's eyes went round, and Ferris softened the corners of his eyes slightly,

"The more dangerous and unknown the place we're going, the more I should be there. If it's me, even if your head flies off, I can save you within one second."

"...Betty is a spirit, so ordinary healing magic doesn't work well on her."

"Yeah, I know. So over these two months, I've proooperly updated it."

"—Seriously?"

"Seriously seriously. —In front of me, I won't let anyone die even if they want to, okay?"

Whether that was truly as extraordinary as it sounded, Beatrice, upon hearing Ferris's reply, made a small sound in her throat as if feeling parched. But from Subaru's perspective, it was truly reassuring.

In fact, ever since the battle with the "Beloved Child" began, there had been a prickling tension in the air at all times, as if they were being watched by blades around the clock.

"And hey, I've been meaning to say this for a while..."

"Hm? What is it, so formal?"

"—"

"Ferris?"

Ferris, who had started to speak himself, fell silent, and Subaru tilted his head. For a while after that, Ferris opened and closed his lips repeatedly, but—

"—You don't need to tiptoe around the thing with me and Crusch-sama every single time."

"Ugh, so you noticed."

"Of course it'd be obvious. ...I do feel sorry for making you worry, though."

"Though?"

"Subaru-kyun, you'd get worked up too if unrelated people said this and that about you and Emilia-sama, right? Think of it as the same thing."

To that analogy, Subaru felt that Ferris wasn't an unrelated person, but since that probably wasn't the point, he held back his objection.

That said, if there were something he could say, he'd want to say it, but—

"...Looks like there's movement at the stump. I'll go get ready."

But mercilessly, just as he'd said what he wanted to say, Ferris noticed the movement on the stump side and turned his back. He hurried back to the dragon carriage, but the reason for that became clear immediately. Crusch was coming over from the direction of the stump.

"Geez, that guy..."

"—? Preparations on this side are complete, but did something happen, 'Ash Star'?"

"Nah, it's nothing, Crusch-san... ah, whoops. 'Verdant Grace,' 'Verdant Grace.'"

When he let his guard down, the code name slipped out, and he hurriedly covered his mouth in regret. At that, Crusch relaxed her lips slightly and continued, "It's fine."

"As long as there are no listening ears around, I'll answer to either name. The ring of 'Verdant Grace' that you gave me is also quite to my liking."

"Well, if the person herself likes it, then I'm glad it wasn't overstepping. At least compared to getting something almost insulting like 'Long Tongue'—that would be a misinterpretation."

"Misinterpretation..."

"It's one of Subaru's mysterious obsessions. It's an occasional illness of his, so no need to worry. More importantly, the crowd clearing was 'White Sheep's' doing again, Betty sees."

"Ah, that's right. As always, 'White Sheep's' negotiation is magnificent. It doesn't feel like cunning in the sense of seasoned craftiness... rather, one simply feels inclined to listen to that person naturally."

"It's the virtue permitted only to fragile, beautiful girls, I guess."

Thinking back, there were quite a number of beautiful people around Subaru alone, but with Emilia at the top, there might be almost no one to whom the expression "fragile" truly suited.

"Come to think of it, everyone feels like beautiful people overflowing with vitality. Could it be that the most mopey and fragile one here is... me...?"

"Hm, I don't understand the reason for your unease, but if anything happens, feel free to rely on me at any time. As long as it's within my power, I will not hesitate to wield this sword."

"A powerful beauty right after saying that...! If I were the heroine, this would be dangerous... ow ow ow."

"Honestly, fooling around again, are we?"

While Subaru's heart fluttered at Crusch's reliability, Beatrice pinched his cheek as punishment. Crusch smiled at the sight of Subaru and Beatrice, then turned back toward the stump.

And then—

"—To visit Flügel's Great Tree with you again. Who would have thought."

"Ah."

"—? What's wrong? Did I say something strange?"

At the surprised voices raised in unison by Subaru and Beatrice, Crusch looked at them with uneasy eyes. At that reaction, Subaru and Beatrice shook their heads together, saying "No, no,"

"It's not that—just now, you see."

"Ferris went off saying the exact same thing as you just did."

"That one, saying the same thing as me... I see. That makes sense."

Her raised eyebrows lasted only a moment before Crusch nodded with immediate understanding.

Naturally, if Subaru, Crusch, and also Ferris came to this place together, what came to mind for all of them was the White Whale subjugation battle. —Wilhelm, who had been the protagonist of that battle, was also one of those whose whereabouts were unknown, having vanished from the capital.

"I heard Wilhelm-san is together with our people. It's worrying, but..."

"No, if it's Wilhelm, he'll overcome a fair amount of hardship. If we're to worry, Father is far more concerning. Being the worrier that he is, he must be under considerable stress."

"—"

"But Father was also a duke before passing the headship to me. In the kingdom's time of crisis, he will stand firm in body and spirit. What we can do is bring the situation to a close as soon as possible."

"I agree. That's what today's plan is for."

Crusch, who trusted in the inner strength of her separated vassals and family, nodded deeply at Beatrice's words. Subaru also nodded back, feeling his resolve tighten anew.

"Don't worry. After all, this place is a lucky spot for us. With the plans we've carried out at Flügel's Great Tree, we've never failed even once."

"That is... fu, naturally."

At Subaru's lighthearted remark delivered with a shrug, Crusch smiled with relaxed shoulders. Winking one eye at that smile, Subaru swore to himself that he would not let that lighthearted remark end as just a lighthearted remark.

Worrying about their companions was the same for Subaru and the others; if the solution to that was saving the kingdom, then he would put the kingdom in checkmate as soon as possible.

For that purpose—

"—Let's go, Beako's family home visit!"

"That way of putting it makes Betty feel inexplicably itchy all over!"

　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△

"—For our part, the top priority is being able to verify the truth of the information obtained. Getting in contact with Roswaal can wait. However..."

"However?"

"If, when we actually need to rely on that knowledge, another alternative means appears, then there will be room for debate as to whether there's any meaning in maintaining a friendly relationship with Roswaal at all."

"Well, you know, friendship isn't about expecting something in return..."

At Subaru's unconvincing follow-up, Russell made a clearly sour face.

Subaru wanted to think that human relationships weren't built solely on calculations of merits and demerits, but it was also difficult to measure the values of the "Six Tongues" commissioner by common sense.

"Moreover, since it's Roswaal we're talking about... I can't even tell what criteria that guy uses to look at people, so I feel like he's in no position to complain about how others see him."

Subaru did think that Roswaal's unexpectedly human side was part of his charm, but the problem lay in the part where he would brazenly trample over that human warmth with his single-minded sense of purpose.

Keeping the friendship issue between Roswaal and Russell in the corner of his mind—

"But is this really okay? We've been trying to avoid conspicuous actions so the enemy wouldn't find us, and now we're clearing out a tourist attraction."

"Needless to say, it's not a desirable situation. No matter how much effort we put in, rumors seep out like raindrops. So we'll likely have only one night to work with."

"One night, huh."

Looking up at the night sky, Subaru clenched his fist at the unreliability of the limited time.

Right now, Beatrice was walking around near the stump of the great tree, searching for the port she sought—a hole in space, like a doorway, connected to the castle through the effect of "Door Crossing." But according to her, it had been hundreds of years since she last left the castle, and the environment around the port should have changed drastically.

"Even though Beako's the only one who can find the key, it's frustrating to have no choice but to leave it to her. Please don't rush, though. But also, hurry without panicking..."

"OI! What're you standing around for! It's been found!"

"Seriously!?"

Just as he was feeling prayerful, Laczins's shout of triumph rang out, and Subaru exchanged glances with Russell before rushing over to where Beatrice had been wrestling with the stump.

Even from a distance, the stump had looked like a small hill, but up close, its extraordinary size stood out even more, making it understandable that it had become a tourist site.

"And with little Beako next to a stump like that, it looks like a scene out of a fairy tale."

"You flatter me. Well, it's true that Betty is a mischievous little devil, like the Tinker Bell from the fairy tales Subaru told her."

"Little devil? I've only ever thought of you as a smart, cute, excellent great spirit. So? So? So?"

"Honestly, you're too excited! Come on, all of you gather around too."

Beatrice, who must have been relieved to have found what she was looking for, beckoned with a smug face that hid that relief—which was adorable—and the group trotted over.

"As expected," "Amazing!" "So it wasn't just talk and attitude after all," "...That's amazing," they praised her in turn, and Beatrice, in a good mood, gave a small cough,

"Honestly, since there was a possibility that the port was set with the great tree as its base point, Betty thought it was fifty-fifty whether it would work. But it's properly here, safe and sound. It's just sleeping."

"Sleeping, you say. Then it's possible to wake it?"

"Of course. —Betty is Mother's greatest masterpiece, after all."

Grinning, Beatrice extended her small hand toward Subaru.

Accepting that as a matter of course, Subaru took her hand and scooped up her light body all at once, standing together with her where she had been standing.

And then—

"Subaru, Betty needs to borrow your power for 'Door Crossing.'"

"Don't be ridiculous. Ever since I brought you out, I've been the one borrowing your power nonstop!"

"—Shall we go?"

From their joined hands and touching bodies, Subaru's mana flowed into Beatrice. —Apparently, it was a rather stubborn sleepyhead, and he could feel his stored mana being consumed all at once.

He'd like it to wake up before Subaru himself withered and dried up, but—

"...I can't help with mana depletion, so don't die, okay."

"Don't jinx it...!"

Perhaps sensing unease at Subaru's rapidly diminishing mana, Ferris had muttered that, and Subaru shot back. —It was right after that.

Suddenly, the stump pulsed. —No, there was a reaction in the space in front of it.

"—"

There wasn't actually a sound.

But everyone present probably felt a great wave of mana from beyond the gate.

Before long, a single line of pale purple light ran through the reacting space, slowly tracing through the air, beginning a drawing of light in that spot.

"This is..."

"—I see. A door."

Crusch voiced the conclusion after her astonishment, and just as she said, the purple light that had begun from Beatrice's raised hand traced through the air, creating a single door of light—a port.

It was unmistakably, perfectly, an entrance to a hidden dungeon.

"What's the condition?"

"It's stable. It just took some effort to wake up; once woken, it should hold for a while. Even if Betty lets go... see, just like this."

Following Beatrice as she withdrew her hand, Subaru also took a step back. Even so, the port that had appeared before them showed no change, nor did it show any sign of vanishing from beneath their feet.

Seeing that, Russell nodded with a "Very good," and—

"—'Black Dog.'"

"Yes yes, I know, I know. Dangerous and risky things are best left to people whose hearts won't ache or burst even if they die, riiight!"

"It's simply putting the right person in the right place. You, too, are a piece that would hurt to lose."

"Ku haha! I'm crying tears of joy and sorrow!"

Waving his hand flippantly, 'Black Dog' stepped forward while spouting sarcasm. He reached his hand toward the port and, without hesitation, moved to open it.

At his back, 'White Sheep' called out to him with a tense voice,

"P-please be careful."

"Good grief, you people... This is that little one's homecoming after years and decades and centuries, right? Don't badmouth her family home so much!"

Even though it was a retort, unlike the one directed at Russell, his words to 'White Sheep' seemed to carry a shade of consideration for her. The relationship between these two was also unclear to him, but despite all their back-and-forth, they did seem to trust each other, which was curious.

At any rate—

"—"

Before everyone's eyes, 'Black Dog' touched the port—and his figure was smoothly swallowed into the other side of the space. Truly, there was an entrance to an invisible space in midair.

'Black Dog's' body did not pass through the other side of the port; he reached somewhere connected across separated space. Then, before Subaru and the others who waited with bated breath, some time passed—and slowly, only his hand returned to this side, raising his thumb firmly.

"That's..."

"Ah, it's a sign that it's safe. A gesture from my hometown."

"'Ash Star' has been getting along well with 'Black Dog' lately, hasn't he?"

"Getting along... is that getting along? Well, knowing he won't bite is progress, I guess."

He hadn't intended it, but seeing the sign made with a finger, he now felt as though 'Black Dog' was being treated like a dog, just as his code name suggested. A large dog like that who wouldn't warm up to anyone would be too dangerous to ever place near Beatrice, though.

In any case—

"If there's no problem, then proceed. —'White Sheep,' you're next."

"Y-yes! Um... here I go!"

Prompted by Russell, 'White Sheep' hesitated slightly, then timidly grasped the hand of 'Black Dog' that was still extended to this side. Instantly, her hand was pulled hard, and with a shriek of "Uhyaa!", her figure also vanished beyond the port.

"Tch, why does I gotta go into a castle connected to a 'Witch'..."

"Stop grumbling, 'Long Tongue.' Your quick wits are valuable. Felt has found herself a fine servant."

"Shut it! I ain't no servant! I'm just a companion by circumstance, a companion!"

And so, Laczins—who had apparently ended up going along with the royal selection by happenstance—and Crusch, who showed no sign of fear, boarded the port. Just before following, Ferris glanced over at Subaru, perhaps concerned about his earlier mana depletion.

"I'm fine, fit as a fiddle. If anything, you look light enough that I could lift you up too."

"No thank you! ...Tell me right away if anything happens."

Leaving just those words behind, Ferris too went beyond the port. In Subaru's arms, Beatrice shifted slightly,

"He said it, did he?"

"He said it. —So, Russell-san, are you going too?"

Watching the group pass through the port one after another, Subaru looked at Russell, the last one remaining.

He was the commissioner of the "Six Tongues," a position that couldn't be replaced. Subaru had asked thinking he surely couldn't go directly to the field himself, but unexpectedly, Russell shook his head,

"If possible, I would like to devote myself to a directing role as well, but when it comes to investigating the research of the 'Witch,' it would be a waste not to use the value of these eyes."

"Ah, the 'Divine Protection of Appraisal.'"

"Yes. When it comes to the research of the 'Witch,' we must be prepared for things that ordinary people cannot comprehend. However, even for things that cannot be understood, these eyes can discern whether they have value, and how much."

"I see... When you put it that way, it's definitely an indispensable skill for a dungeon raid."

In games like dungeon-crawling hack-and-slash, it's common to bring back unidentified items and not know whether they're valuable until checking them on the surface, but Russell's divine protection could at least determine the value of those items.

Being able to avoid grabbing things at random or wasting effort for nothing was extremely welcome.

"So I will leave securing this location to other personnel and accompany you."

With that, Russell snapped his fingers and sent a complex hand signal toward the dragon carriage.

It seemed to be a code unique to the "Six Tongues," and since Subaru occasionally saw it being used, he'd been trying to learn it as appropriate, but it wasn't going well.

"It would be troublesome if it were easy to learn, so that's correct. Though those who have no intention of learning, like 'Black Dog,' are also troublesome."

"'Black Dog' seemed to take a liking to Subaru's hand signs, so it might be a problem of how it's taught. Or maybe a problem with who's teaching."

"Good grief. —Well then, I'll go on ahead."

As if plugging his ears to inconvenient talk, Russell passed through the port first and crossed over.

Now, the only ones left in front of the stump were Subaru and Beatrice. They could just as well chase right after the backs of those who'd gone ahead, but—

"—I can't wait four hundred years, but shall we wait about three hundred until you've sorted out your feelings?"

"...Honestly, you're too considerate. Betty's only a tiny bit nervous."

Beatrice, in his arms, relaxed her slightly stiffened body and smiled softly. Then, gazing at the front door of the home she hadn't returned to in four hundred years,

"—Now!"

"Right on!"

Pumping himself up with meaningless energy, Subaru, holding Beatrice, hopped into the port.

In that instant, there was a faint sensation of passing through a thin curtain of light, and the grassy air of the Lifaus Plains receded behind them—instead, cold, dry wind greeted the two of them.

"—"

Under his landing soles was the sensation of a stone floor.

Immediately behind them, the port connected to the great tree's remains was open just as it had been on the entrance side, and the six who had entered before were standing a short distance away, looking around.

It was dark, with no light source. The only reason they could see their companions who'd entered first was that 'Black Dog' and Laczins had lit flames at their fingertips as makeshift lights.

Was this a castle? The air seemed to carry a unique weight, though whether that was because it was the home of a "Witch," or the weight of four hundred years of silence, he couldn't tell.

"So this is..."

"Kugelschreiber Castle."

Subaru murmured in awe, and gently lowered Beatrice, who patted his shoulder, to the floor.

Stepping on the castle floor with her own feet, Beatrice walked. The sound of her shoes echoed through the castle interior, and the walls and pillars buried in darkness, the long corridors stretching left and right, carried the reverberations far away.

As if welcoming the return of a resident after four hundred years.

"—"

Closing her eyes, Beatrice took in the castle's air with her whole small body.

Grateful to the others for respecting Beatrice's feelings by not calling out to her, Subaru, on behalf of the silent castle, placed a hand gently on Beatrice's head.

And then—

"Welcome home, Beako."

"—. I'm home."

As if responding to that greeting, the castle walls suddenly began to emit a faint light.

Hastily stopping the others with his hand as they held their breath, Subaru looked around and found the faint light familiar—it reminded him of the tomb in the "Sanctuary," whose interior had glowed the same way.

Indeed, as one would expect of a castle bearing the name of the "Witch of Greed," there seemed to be an exceptional number of commonalities.

The castle. —It was unmistakably a castle.

However—

"Still though... it's pretty run-down."

The darkness was driven back by the light, illuminating the castle's interior more clearly than moonlight. —The interior that became visible was, to put it kindly, not in a state fit to receive guests.

The walls were caved in as if crushed by a giant palm, and some of the thick pillars had been forcibly bent partway. The ceiling was heavily gouged out, and shattered stone had been compressed together and inexplicably rolled into spheres. The corridor leading deeper into the castle was crushed halfway on one side, and neither doors, nor windows, nor even the floor could maintain their original forms.

"This isn't the kind of deterioration from being left alone for a long time. Unless someone was extremely bad at cleaning up, maybe this is the reason Beako left the castle with her mother?"

"That's about right. Mother certainly wasn't very good at cleaning up, but this is different from that. —It's the work of the 'Warlock of Melancholy.'"

"The Warlock of Melancholy... that's, if I remember—"

It was a name he had heard before from Ryuzu Shima, the former administrator of the "Sanctuary."

The being that had been the trigger for the creation of the barrier in the "Sanctuary," and the cause of Beatrice's parting with her first friend. —A hidden extra great sin, absent from history.

"Subaru heard about it from Ryuzu too. —The 'Warlock of Melancholy,' Hector, was a longtime enemy of Betty, Mother, and also Roswaal, as a side note. He was obsessed with Mother and followed her around endlessly."

"Following her around... a stalker of a 'Witch'—what an outrageous person. Did he fall that hard for your mother?"

"In terms of warped affection, maybe so. But it wasn't about romance or anything. After all, Hector was Mother's father."

"—"

At that, Subaru gasped in unexpected surprise. The fingers he instinctively pressed to his chest touched the black sphere hanging from his neck, and he found himself gripping it.

—The "Warlock of Melancholy" Hector was the father of the "Witch of Greed" Echidna.

"The more information I get, the more this Hector sounds like an absolute monster. ...Ah, but even when I say Echidna, it's the Echidna on Beako's side, so that's fine...? No, damn, this is confusing. Damn it, Echidna...!"

"No need to get all flustered. You don't need to worry—Hector... Mother's father is already nowhere to be found. Not just her father—her mother too."

"The way you say that, the mother sounds like she has quite the story too..."

"She's a 'Witch.' —The 'Witch of Vanity,' Pandora. That's Mother's mother."

Saying it as if it were nothing, Beatrice walked on with small steps.

Honestly, Subaru was quite shocked by this new information.

"Her father was 'Melancholy' and her mother was 'Vanity'..."

Both were sins of humanity that had been included among the old great sins, before the seven deadly sins took their current form.

He had heard that there were those who bore those past great sins, but to think that both of them were connected to Echidna—that she was the child of a "Witch" and a "Warlock"—indeed, one could say it made sense.

Come to think of it, Echidna had seemed particularly fixated on the concept of "Witches" at the tea parties. Whether that was related to her origins was uncertain, but—

"...I really should have talked with you more."

Regretting an opportunity that would never come again, Subaru muttered that.

Leaving Subaru to his thoughts, Beatrice walked ahead to where there was an extraordinarily large hole in the floor, where 'White Sheep' was timidly peering in.

"'White Sheep,' do be careful—if you accidentally peek and fall in, it won't be a laughing matter."

Standing beside 'White Sheep,' Beatrice kicked a piece of stone at her feet into the hole. —But no sound of the stone hitting the bottom could be heard.

At that fact, not only 'White Sheep' but everyone else's eyes went wide, and Beatrice put her hands on her hips, turning back to face the group,

"This is Kugelschreiber Castle... the home of a 'Witch,' floating in the sky of a separate space. If you fall, you will absolutely never come back, so everyone must walk with care."

Thus she lectured them on how to walk through her family home of four hundred years.
